HOW DO I USE GUNZ?

1. Define Your System

Choose the parameters that describe your discrete-clock system:

quantum
What it means: The smallest time step (in base time units).
Example: 1,000,000 (1 ms, since the system timer has 1ms granularity, and we work in nanoseconds — time_unit = 9)

target_ticks
What it means: The number of ticks you need to reach (the "finish line").
Example: 1140 (the animation block length)

mult_num / mult_den
What it means: The advance multiplier (e.g., ticks per quantum).
Example: mult_num=48, mult_den=10 (4.8 ticks per ms)

round_mode
What it means: How to round fractional ticks.
Example: 1 (half-to-even, the default)

hold_cycles / sep_cycles
What it means: Extra cycles after the target.
Example: 0 (none)

time_unit
What it means: The base time scale.
Example: 9 (nanoseconds)

reference_block_time
What it means: The ideal block time you expect (the "truth anchor").
Example: 248,000,000 (248 ms in nanoseconds)

jitter_weight
What it means: How much to penalise timing jitter.
Example: 4 (default)

advance_regularity_weight
What it means: How much to penalise low advance regularity.
Example: 1 (neutral, default)

jitter_window_radius
What it means: The radius of the jitter window. If set to 0, the default radius is 10000 (21 points, ±0.1 Hz). If non‑zero, the actual radius in 10⁻⁵ Hz units is this value multiplied by 1000.
Example: 0 (use default)

Note: The quantum value must be expressed in the same base time unit as time_unit. For example, if time_unit = 9 (nanoseconds), a 1 ms clock granularity is quantum = 1,000,000. If you prefer to keep quantum = 1, you must set time_unit = 3 (milliseconds) and express all values in milliseconds.

The reference_source field (0=explicit, 1=guessed, 2=calibrated, 3=overflow-clamped explicit) records the provenance of your reference_block_time.

2. Choose a Frequency Range

Decide which frequency interval to scan and how finely (the step size). A "zone" is just a start-to-end frequency range in 10⁻⁵ Hz units.

- min_freq = 20000000 (200.00000 Hz)
- max_freq = 30000000 (300.00000 Hz)
- step_freq = 1 (the default, 10⁻⁵ Hz resolution)

Zones may now start as low as 0.00001 Hz (freq_low = 1). The jitter window automatically skips invalid points near zero.

You can also use SCAN_RES to set a different step size, or SKIP_ZONE to skip evaluating a zone entirely (useful for gaps you already know are bad). All zones in a program must be strictly disjoint—they cannot overlap.

3. Run the Scan

A GUNZ runtime processes the zones and writes the map and metadata files.

- Minimal Profile: You can build a tiny, fully conformant scanner that only handles static zones and spits out a valid .gunzmap. No bytecode VM, no callbacks, no sandboxing required. This is the recommended starting point for embedded systems or fixed-range sweeps.

- Full Profile: For AI-driven exploration, you can use the bytecode VM, dynamic opcodes, and callbacks (PURE_CALL) to generate scan programs dynamically.

The GUNZ 3.35.0 specification is complete. You can start with a minimal implementation—just the static lane, a zone evaluator, and map output—and be fully conformant.

4. Read the Map

The map shows which frequencies are sweet spots (low jitter, stable), which are detrimental (high jitter, flagged with FLAG_AVOID), and where there are large gaps (emitted as long-jump entries).

- Sweet spots: low block_time_inaccuracy, low jitter, and wide stability_width.
- Detrimental zones: high jitter or high advance_regularity (converted to base time units) exceeding the thresholds.
- Long-jump entries: gaps larger than ±0.32767 Hz are compressed into a single entry. The sentinel is run_length == 0 and block_time_inaccuracy == 0; the gap itself is stored as a signed 64-bit integer in bytes 8–15 of the entry. No other field in that range (flags, jitter, etc.) is interpreted separately.

The step_freq field in the map header tells you the actual resolution. If it is 0xFFFF, you must look at the .gunzmeta file for the true step size; otherwise 0 means the default step of 1.

5. Adjust and Repeat

Use time_unit to coarsen or refine the scan, tune the jitter window radius or jitter weight, and chain multiple maps with fingerprint links.

- Coarse scans (large time_unit, e.g., 3 = milliseconds) are fast and reveal major edges.
- Fine scans (small time_unit, e.g., 9 = nanoseconds) are more accurate but more expensive.
- Adaptive multi-resolution scanning is a core design pattern: start coarse, deepen when silent, ascend when costly, verify upward, and re-probe if no edge appears.

Each scan is a checkpoint. The .gunzmeta records exactly which truth anchor and scan parameters were used, and can optionally link to the previous checkpoint via a fingerprint chain (chain_next_fingerprint and chain_sequence). This lets you explore without fear—if a new configuration makes things worse, reload the last good checkpoint (using the fingerprint link) and try a different path. Over many iterations, each map becomes the anchor for the next, and GUNZ turns from a one-time scanner into a truth-seeking compass.

