SUMMARY: GUNZ IN A NUTSHELL

Discrete-clock system: Any process that advances in whole ticks (e.g., game engine, hardware timer, motor controller).

Resonance map: A compact table (16 bytes/entry) showing where frequencies are stable, where they jitter, and which to avoid.

Truth anchor: The reference_block_time—the ideal you are trying to achieve. Provenance is tracked (explicit, guessed, calibrated, overflow-clamped).

Jitter: Block-time variation when you nudge the frequency slightly. Measured in base time units.

Advance regularity: Per-cycle advance consistency. High regularity = stable, predictable cycles. Low regularity = variation. Measured in ticks × 100.

Stability width: The width of the stable region around a sweet spot. Wider = more robust to drift.

Phoenix mechanism: Each scan is a checkpoint. Chain maps via fingerprints to create a persistent memory of exploration.

Minimal Profile: Build a tiny, fully conformant scanner with just the static lane and map output. No VM, no callbacks, no sandboxing.

---

That is GUNZ in a nutshell: a clock-domain resonance scanner that gives you a map of truth, one frequency at a time. The map is the verdict. The metadata is the story. The fingerprint is the seal.
